1
00:00:55,988 --> 00:00:57,907
Stay where you are and put
your hands in the air.

2
00:01:00,368 --> 00:01:02,870
My safe.
Did you open my safe?

3
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:08,709
Stop!

4
00:01:24,934 --> 00:01:26,310
Hands behind your back.

5
00:01:27,144 --> 00:01:28,729
- On your feet now.
- Get up.

6
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:30,856
Come on.

7
00:01:43,202 --> 00:01:44,245
Thanks.

8
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:47,289
Just a couple bucks, buddy.
Help a guy out.

9
00:01:47,415 --> 00:01:49,625
Just need to know if you saw
anything, sir, anything at all.

10
00:01:54,964 --> 00:01:56,006
I don't know.

11
00:01:57,466 --> 00:01:59,844
Maybe if you tell me
what you're looking for

12
00:02:00,386 --> 00:02:01,846
I could help you out.

13
00:02:02,054 --> 00:02:04,014
I don't know.
Loud noise, gunshot.

14
00:02:04,515 --> 00:02:07,476
Did you see anyone unusual?
Anyone acting unusually?

15
00:02:07,685 --> 00:02:08,728
Maybe I did.

16
00:02:10,855 --> 00:02:11,897
Or...

17
00:02:12,648 --> 00:02:13,941
Or maybe I didn't.

18
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:18,863
You give me a couple bucks
maybe help me remember better.

19
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:24,869
- You got 2 bucks?
- No. You?

20
00:02:25,244 --> 00:02:26,620
Looks like a robbery gone bad.

21
00:02:27,204 --> 00:02:29,373
Victim's name was Ted Fisher.
He owned the place.

22
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:31,250
Something's off here.

23
00:02:32,126 --> 00:02:33,627
The alarm was set off during the break-in

24
00:02:33,753 --> 00:02:36,005
and the killer still took the time
to tear the place apart?

25
00:02:36,464 --> 00:02:38,090
Watch the blood. Rug's pretty soaked.

26
00:02:45,347 --> 00:02:47,433
And if the killer
broke the door entering

27
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,019
why is the victim over here
with his back turned?

28
00:02:50,394 --> 00:02:52,396
- Something's off here.
- That's very astute, Lisbon.

29
00:02:52,521 --> 00:02:55,483
- There's more here than meets the eye.
- There you are. Where have you been?

30
00:02:55,691 --> 00:02:57,902
I haven't been anywhere.
I was out back checking escape routes.

31
00:02:58,194 --> 00:02:59,820
Really? Any clues?

32
00:03:00,362 --> 00:03:02,448
Only that the murderer didn't find
what he was looking for.

33
00:03:03,407 --> 00:03:05,034
And... how do you know that?

34
00:03:05,242 --> 00:03:07,328
Because this whole room
has been torn apart.

35
00:03:08,037 --> 00:03:10,164
You find what you're looking for,
you stop the search, right?

36
00:03:10,456 --> 00:03:12,166
So part of this room
would be untouched.

37
00:03:12,917 --> 00:03:14,794
Unless he found it in the very last
place he was looking.

38
00:03:15,044 --> 00:03:16,086
Do you know the probability

39
00:03:16,212 --> 00:03:18,506
of finding something
in the last possible place?

40
00:03:19,048 --> 00:03:20,841
It's extremely improbable.

41
00:03:21,967 --> 00:03:24,929
- I'll see you two back at the office.
- Why are you in a hurry?

42
00:03:25,221 --> 00:03:28,474
Well, as much as I enjoy these
lovely surroundings, Lisbon

43
00:03:29,892 --> 00:03:30,935
we have a job to do.

44
00:03:32,728 --> 00:03:34,021
Am I wrong?

45
00:03:35,815 --> 00:03:36,857
- Bye.
- Bye.

46
00:03:44,740 --> 00:03:47,284
Hey, sir. What are you doing here
so late on a Friday night?

47
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:50,162
My house was burglarized.
I caught him red-handed.

48
00:03:50,454 --> 00:03:52,081
Whoa. You want one of my people
to question him?

49
00:03:52,289 --> 00:03:54,708
No. Sac P.D. can handle it.
It's a slam dunk.

50
00:03:55,376 --> 00:03:57,294
Come Monday, when court opens

51
00:03:57,753 --> 00:04:01,674
the man who broke into my house
will be very sorry that he did.

52
00:04:16,272 --> 00:04:17,314
Donny Culpepper.

53
00:04:17,857 --> 00:04:18,941
What a surprise to see you here.

54
00:04:19,567 --> 00:04:20,943
Monday I go before a judge.

55
00:04:21,193 --> 00:04:22,611
You got till then
to get me outta here.

56
00:04:22,945 --> 00:04:26,657
Yeah, well, um, that could be
a little problematic.

57
00:04:26,782 --> 00:04:30,703
Otherwise I will tell everyone how
you hired me to rob your boss' house.

58
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:34,707
Well, that's just snitching.
Isn't that against your...

59
00:04:35,541 --> 00:04:37,793
- Code of honor?
- No.

60
00:04:51,638 --> 00:04:52,806
I paid you very well

61
00:04:52,973 --> 00:04:54,892
on the understanding
that you wouldn't get caught.

62
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:57,394
- Did you at least get what I wanted?
- No.

63
00:04:57,686 --> 00:05:00,022
I looked everywhere.
Got into his safe. Nothing.

64
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:03,358
Just some old coins, a watch
and a Tupperware, like, tub thing.

65
00:05:03,775 --> 00:05:07,112
Tupperware. Did Laroche say
anything when he found you?

66
00:05:07,905 --> 00:05:09,656
He wanted to know
if I got into the safe.

67
00:05:09,865 --> 00:05:10,991
- What'd you tell him?
- Nothin'.

68
00:05:11,158 --> 00:05:13,035
I threw somethin' at his head and took off.

69
00:05:13,952 --> 00:05:16,079
{\an8}Yeah, you really blew this.
I thought you were a professional.

70
00:05:16,371 --> 00:05:19,291
{\an8}Don't talk down to me.
If you don't get me outta here.

71
00:05:19,541 --> 00:05:21,627
By Monday, you're the one
who's gonna have to pay for it.

72
00:05:21,752 --> 00:05:22,794
Trust me on that.

73
00:05:22,961 --> 00:05:25,505
{\an8}Trust. Yeah, really off on the good foot
with trust, are we?

74
00:05:27,799 --> 00:05:29,301
{\an8}- Hi.
- "Where are you?"

75
00:05:29,426 --> 00:05:31,345
{\an8}I thought you wanted
to get working on this case.

76
00:05:31,595 --> 00:05:34,097
{\an8}"I-I do. I just ran across the street"

77
00:05:34,222 --> 00:05:36,433
to the, uh, fruit place
to pick up some fresh fruit.

78
00:05:36,642 --> 00:05:38,143
{\an8}"Flavonoids... lt's very good
for logical reasoning"

79
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:42,773
{\an8}- "and visual memory, you know?"
- Very interesting. Get here quick.

80
00:05:43,106 --> 00:05:44,483
Yep. I'm on my way.

81
00:05:47,069 --> 00:05:48,987
The clock's running, starting now.

82
00:05:52,866 --> 00:05:54,952
You close your eyes
and count to a hundred.

83
00:05:56,244 --> 00:05:57,287
{\an8}Thank you.

84
00:06:00,207 --> 00:06:02,834
{\an8}Fisher owned the repair shop
about a year and a half.

85
00:06:03,251 --> 00:06:05,212
{\an8}Seemed like a good citizen...
Volunteer work

86
00:06:05,462 --> 00:06:07,422
{\an8}with underprivileged kids
and the homeless.

87
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:08,924
{\an8}Dig a little deeper.

88
00:06:09,049 --> 00:06:10,676
{\an8}Find out what he did before
he bought the shop.

89
00:06:10,801 --> 00:06:11,843
Will do.

90
00:06:12,302 --> 00:06:14,262
Hey, boss. Just talked
to Fisher's girlfriend.

91
00:06:14,554 --> 00:06:15,639
{\an8}She was at the morgue,
I.D.ing the body.

92
00:06:15,764 --> 00:06:18,642
{\an8}Kinda upset, but she agreed to meet us
at the apartment she shared with Fisher.

93
00:06:18,767 --> 00:06:19,893
- All right, let's go.
- Okay.

94
00:06:20,978 --> 00:06:22,604
{\an8}Agent Lisbon, a word?

95
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,776
{\an8}I just discovered that
you investigated my burglar

96
00:06:28,151 --> 00:06:31,071
{\an8}for a murder about a year ago...
Donny Culpepper.

97
00:06:31,363 --> 00:06:32,739
{\an8}- Culpepper?
- Donny Culpepper.

98
00:06:32,864 --> 00:06:34,241
{\an8}I remember him.
He threatened to kill me.

99
00:06:34,408 --> 00:06:36,159
You're gonna have to narrow it down
a little bit more than that.

100
00:06:36,326 --> 00:06:37,369
{\an8}The jewelry store case.

101
00:06:37,494 --> 00:06:39,246
{\an8}We picked him up, found him in the hot tub.

102
00:06:39,746 --> 00:06:40,872
{\an8}I wonder if there's a connection.

103
00:06:41,039 --> 00:06:45,377
{\an8}- He's a criminal. He does wrong things.
- Yeah, probably just a coincidence then.

104
00:06:46,753 --> 00:06:48,922
{\an8}Yeah. Probably.

105
00:06:51,133 --> 00:06:52,175
{\an8}I'll let you know.

106
00:06:55,137 --> 00:06:56,930
- Is there a connection?
- No.

107
00:06:59,266 --> 00:07:00,559
Absolutely not.

108
00:07:03,854 --> 00:07:05,981
Oh, damn. I forgot something.
Uh, I'll meet you there.

109
00:07:06,148 --> 00:07:07,190
Forgot what?

110
00:07:07,315 --> 00:07:09,484
My, frum baho llym or gansterning.

111
00:07:19,745 --> 00:07:20,787
Miss blue?

112
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:23,957
I'm agent Lisbon.
This is agent Rigsby.

113
00:07:24,416 --> 00:07:25,709
We're really sorry for your loss.

114
00:07:26,793 --> 00:07:28,670
Just have a couple questions for you.

115
00:07:29,087 --> 00:07:30,130
Okay. Sure.

116
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:32,549
Just a few small things.
Where were you last night?

117
00:07:32,924 --> 00:07:35,635
When it happened?
Home, asleep.

118
00:07:36,178 --> 00:07:38,138
I had the early shift
at the animal shelter.

119
00:07:38,722 --> 00:07:39,806
Do you mind if we talk inside?

120
00:07:40,974 --> 00:07:42,017
Okay.

121
00:07:42,517 --> 00:07:45,353
Did your boyfriend Mr. Fisher
work that late very often?

122
00:07:45,896 --> 00:07:48,356
Sometimes. If he had
a big project he had to finish.

123
00:07:48,774 --> 00:07:51,068
But it's not like work
was his life or anything.

124
00:07:51,276 --> 00:07:52,319
He was in balance.

125
00:07:52,819 --> 00:07:54,863
Right. And, how long ago did you meet him?

126
00:07:55,072 --> 00:07:57,240
About a year ago.
We met at yoga.

127
00:07:59,117 --> 00:08:00,368
That's... weird.

128
00:08:01,078 --> 00:08:02,370
I'm sure I locked that.

129
00:08:03,038 --> 00:08:04,081
Stand back, ma'am.

130
00:08:15,133 --> 00:08:16,176
Clear.

131
00:08:25,352 --> 00:08:26,394
Clear.

132
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:29,272
Jane was right.

133
00:08:29,397 --> 00:08:30,440
Looks like somebody wanted
something Fisher had.

134
00:08:30,565 --> 00:08:32,192
Call it in.
Put somebody to watch the shop.

135
00:08:32,359 --> 00:08:33,568
If Jane is right, maybe
they'll go back there.

136
00:08:33,693 --> 00:08:34,736
Okay, you got it.

137
00:08:36,321 --> 00:08:38,281
I can't believe
this level of hostility.

138
00:08:39,866 --> 00:08:41,159
There's nothing here
worth stealing.

139
00:08:41,785 --> 00:08:42,828
Nothin'?

140
00:08:43,161 --> 00:08:44,996
Ted and I weren't really
into material stuff.

141
00:08:45,622 --> 00:08:47,707
He spent, like, eight years
in the Peace Corps.

142
00:08:48,125 --> 00:08:49,167
Oh, my gosh.

143
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:52,003
Oh, excuse me.

144
00:08:52,712 --> 00:08:55,465
I'm sorry.
Ma'am, can I ask you a question?

145
00:08:55,715 --> 00:08:57,384
Oh, I'm sorry.
I don't mean to be intrusive.

146
00:08:57,509 --> 00:08:58,635
I live upstairs.

147
00:08:58,844 --> 00:09:01,555
There was a break-in here.
Did you see or hear anything?

148
00:09:01,847 --> 00:09:04,141
No. A break-in?
I was at the market.

149
00:09:04,349 --> 00:09:05,642
Did they steal anything?

150
00:09:05,851 --> 00:09:06,893
Thank you for your time.

151
00:09:07,018 --> 00:09:09,062
If you think of anything
you can give me a call at this number.

152
00:09:09,479 --> 00:09:10,522
All right.

153
00:09:19,156 --> 00:09:20,532
Okay. Thanks.

154
00:09:21,158 --> 00:09:22,909
Sac P.D. put a man on the store.

155
00:09:23,785 --> 00:09:24,828
Check on the girlfriend.

156
00:09:24,995 --> 00:09:27,122
She's got no real alibi
other than being a hippie.

157
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:37,090
- Something bugging you, boss?
- Behind us. Dark late model sedan.

158
00:09:38,383 --> 00:09:40,218
I'm pretty sure I saw it
outside the repair shop.

159
00:09:40,343 --> 00:09:42,554
- I think it's following us.
- Got it.

160
00:09:46,057 --> 00:09:48,518
And there it goes.
It's probably nothing.

161
00:09:48,977 --> 00:09:50,020
Just a look-alike.

162
00:09:50,187 --> 00:09:52,647
Maybe. Run the plates.
See if you find anything.

163
00:09:56,860 --> 00:09:57,903
Hey.

164
00:09:59,321 --> 00:10:01,114
You're new to me.
Uh, who...Who are you now?

165
00:10:02,115 --> 00:10:03,992
Detention enforcement officer
O'Donnell.

166
00:10:04,451 --> 00:10:06,244
Guarding the prisoner up to
and until transport.

167
00:10:07,245 --> 00:10:08,997
Gung ho. Good to see.

168
00:10:09,623 --> 00:10:10,957
Well, welcome, O'Donnell.

169
00:10:11,082 --> 00:10:13,084
I'm Patrick Jane.
I work with the CBI.

170
00:10:14,586 --> 00:10:15,670
Okay.
No worries.

171
00:10:16,004 --> 00:10:19,090
Did you...Did you lose this?

172
00:10:19,799 --> 00:10:21,968
A quarter.
I don't think so.

173
00:10:22,928 --> 00:10:27,599
Well, it's less of a quarter
more of a good luck piece, really.

174
00:10:27,849 --> 00:10:30,060
I mean, take a closer look.
Look at the way the light hits it.

175
00:10:30,894 --> 00:10:32,354
Shines and gleams

176
00:10:33,146 --> 00:10:34,272
like liquid sunshine.

177
00:10:35,232 --> 00:10:36,358
Keep looking at it.

178
00:10:37,400 --> 00:10:39,486
It's kind of soothing
the way it shines, isn't it?

179
00:10:39,611 --> 00:10:41,529
You can feel your worries
and responsibilities

180
00:10:41,696 --> 00:10:44,491
just... fading away.

181
00:10:45,742 --> 00:10:48,328
You might even want to close your eyes
and just have a little sleep.

182
00:10:51,873 --> 00:10:53,458
My Uncle had a buffalo nickel.

183
00:10:54,709 --> 00:10:56,336
He was always gonna sell it
and buy a ranch and all that

184
00:10:56,503 --> 00:10:58,255
but he lost it in a poker game.

185
00:10:58,880 --> 00:10:59,923
So not yours, huh?

186
00:11:00,423 --> 00:11:02,801
No. Maybe check upstairs.

187
00:11:03,802 --> 00:11:04,886
Will do. Thank you.

188
00:11:09,766 --> 00:11:11,935
I checked up on the girlfriend.
She seems clean.

189
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:13,895
No priors, committed pacifist.

190
00:11:14,020 --> 00:11:15,772
People I talked to say she
and Fisher were close.

191
00:11:16,064 --> 00:11:17,482
All right. Let's keep an eye on her, though.

192
00:11:17,607 --> 00:11:18,650
Okay.

193
00:11:18,775 --> 00:11:20,610
Hey. I pulled a message
off the victim's cell phone.

194
00:11:20,735 --> 00:11:21,778
Think you wanna hear it.

195
00:11:22,404 --> 00:11:23,571
"Fisher, you dog!"

196
00:11:23,697 --> 00:11:26,032
"You think you can get away with this?
I find you, Fisher!"

197
00:11:26,199 --> 00:11:28,910
"You're gonna pay for what you done!
You hear me, Fisher?! You pay!"

198
00:11:29,494 --> 00:11:31,663
- He doesn't sound very happy.
- What's the accent?

199
00:11:32,414 --> 00:11:34,457
I sent a copy down to Pete in forensics.
He can tell us.

200
00:11:34,582 --> 00:11:35,625
He's a genius with accents.

201
00:11:35,834 --> 00:11:36,918
What time did the call come in?

202
00:11:37,127 --> 00:11:38,295
11:45 last night.

203
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:41,214
Coroner puts time of death
between 11:00 and 2:00. Bingo.

204
00:11:41,673 --> 00:11:42,716
Maybe.

205
00:11:42,841 --> 00:11:43,883
I'm working on getting the subscriber info

206
00:11:44,009 --> 00:11:45,343
for the number from the phone
company right now.

207
00:11:45,468 --> 00:11:46,970
- Good.
- Oh, boss, one more thing.

208
00:11:47,095 --> 00:11:50,056
I've been looking into Fisher's past
and hit a snag with the Peace Corps thing.

209
00:11:50,265 --> 00:11:51,308
What kind of snag?

210
00:11:51,433 --> 00:11:54,853
Well, they've got no record of Fisher
being a member going back ten years.

211
00:11:55,437 --> 00:11:56,479
I'm having them check to see

212
00:11:56,604 --> 00:11:58,315
if it could be a clerical error
or spelling mistake.

213
00:11:58,440 --> 00:12:00,191
Talk to the girlfriend.
Find out where he was stationed.

214
00:12:01,151 --> 00:12:02,193
All right.

215
00:12:03,778 --> 00:12:05,947
Are you taking a vacation
from this case or what?

216
00:12:07,073 --> 00:12:08,116
Huh? No.

217
00:12:08,825 --> 00:12:12,203
Boss. I put the license of the car that you
thought was following us in the system.

218
00:12:12,537 --> 00:12:14,247
It was rented from the airport
three days ago.

219
00:12:14,414 --> 00:12:16,458
Corporate account to some shell company
I couldn't track.

220
00:12:16,708 --> 00:12:17,751
I just got a fresh hit.

221
00:12:17,917 --> 00:12:20,837
The car was ticketed a half-hour ago just two
blocks away from the victim's repair shop.

222
00:12:20,962 --> 00:12:22,130
Nice. Let's go.

223
00:12:24,132 --> 00:12:25,175
You coming or what?

224
00:12:25,842 --> 00:12:27,218
Are those bulletproof vests?

225
00:12:27,552 --> 00:12:29,637
- Yeah.
- Think not.

226
00:12:31,264 --> 00:12:32,682
- Good luck.
- Okay.

227
00:13:02,462 --> 00:13:03,505
No light.

228
00:13:27,278 --> 00:13:28,321
Locked.

229
00:13:49,634 --> 00:13:50,677
Freeze!

230
00:13:50,844 --> 00:13:52,262
- Drop your weapons!
- Put your hands on your heads!

231
00:13:56,808 --> 00:13:57,976
Hello!

232
00:14:16,492 --> 00:14:19,745
Okay. So yesterday
you were bumming change.

233
00:14:21,105 --> 00:14:22,899
Here you are today
with a $1,000 handgun

234
00:14:23,691 --> 00:14:25,109
nicely maintained.

235
00:14:25,693 --> 00:14:27,320
You can understand
our puzzlement.

236
00:14:30,573 --> 00:14:32,200
Sir, my name is Cole Ruger.

237
00:14:33,117 --> 00:14:35,203
I have a license for that
and a carry permit.

238
00:14:37,497 --> 00:14:38,539
Okay, Mr. Ruger

239
00:14:39,290 --> 00:14:40,750
what do you know about the
death of Ted Fisher?

240
00:14:41,042 --> 00:14:42,085
Nothing.

241
00:14:42,627 --> 00:14:44,670
Fisher was a buddy of mine.
That's all.

242
00:14:45,254 --> 00:14:46,798
You and he were
in the Peace Corps together?

243
00:14:48,174 --> 00:14:49,217
No.

244
00:14:49,759 --> 00:14:51,302
We worked at Sagaris together.

245
00:14:52,303 --> 00:14:53,471
What is Sagaris?

246
00:14:54,597 --> 00:14:55,723
A security firm.

247
00:14:56,682 --> 00:15:00,228
We provide services
the regular army isn't equipped for.

248
00:15:02,271 --> 00:15:04,524
Wait. You and Fisher
were mercenaries?

249
00:15:05,191 --> 00:15:07,402
Private security contractors.

250
00:15:08,444 --> 00:15:10,238
Where did you do
most of your contracting?

251
00:15:10,696 --> 00:15:13,116
All over. Mostly Iraq.
Fallujah, Basra.

252
00:15:15,701 --> 00:15:16,911
Where were you
when Fisher was killed?

253
00:15:17,537 --> 00:15:20,081
Ted called me two days ago
and said he was in trouble.

254
00:15:20,706 --> 00:15:21,749
He needed help.

255
00:15:22,041 --> 00:15:24,210
Whatever it was
he couldn't say it over the phone.

256
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,963
And you just dropped everything
no questions asked, headed here?

257
00:15:27,171 --> 00:15:29,465
That's right. Wouldn't you?

258
00:15:30,383 --> 00:15:31,509
Where were you coming from?

259
00:15:32,802 --> 00:15:33,970
I was overseas.

260
00:15:34,762 --> 00:15:36,514
What did Fisher tell you
when you got here?

261
00:15:37,265 --> 00:15:38,307
Nothing.

262
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:40,643
He was already dead.

263
00:15:42,437 --> 00:15:43,646
I was too late.

264
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:46,190
At which point you disguised yourself
as a homeless man

265
00:15:46,315 --> 00:15:47,567
to get close to the crime scene.

266
00:15:48,192 --> 00:15:51,112
I was trying to gather intel,
figure out who killed Ted.

267
00:15:51,863 --> 00:15:53,948
Which is why you broke into his store
with a loaded handgun?

268
00:15:54,157 --> 00:15:56,159
I was staking out the place.
I heard a noise inside.

269
00:15:56,325 --> 00:15:57,743
The back door was open. I went in.

270
00:15:58,870 --> 00:16:00,746
I encountered that woman.
She was armed.

271
00:16:01,330 --> 00:16:02,373
So I drew my weapon.

272
00:16:02,498 --> 00:16:04,542
Like I said, it's fully licensed
and I have a carry permit.

273
00:16:07,962 --> 00:16:09,005
Look...

274
00:16:09,297 --> 00:16:13,676
Agent, I wanna work with you guys
to find out who killed Ted.

275
00:16:14,594 --> 00:16:16,512
Whatever I can do. So as soon as
you cut me loose...

276
00:16:16,637 --> 00:16:18,181
- Cut you loose?
- Sure.

277
00:16:20,349 --> 00:16:21,642
I haven't committed a crime.

278
00:16:23,311 --> 00:16:24,770
Or should I call my attorney?

279
00:16:30,735 --> 00:16:33,321
Okay. Obviously, you're
not the victim's neighbor

280
00:16:33,529 --> 00:16:35,573
and I need you to explain this.

281
00:16:37,825 --> 00:16:39,076
My name is Vivian Griswold.

282
00:16:39,368 --> 00:16:41,913
I had nothing to do with
the murder of Ted Fisher.

283
00:16:42,288 --> 00:16:45,291
A client hired me six months ago
to observe Mr. Fisher.

284
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:47,835
What kind of client?
What exactly do you do?

285
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:51,756
I work for a company
providing corporate intelligence

286
00:16:52,048 --> 00:16:53,549
and covert surveillance.

287
00:16:53,799 --> 00:16:56,552
A private sector spy.
Who's the client?

288
00:16:57,220 --> 00:16:59,138
Can't tell you that.

289
00:17:00,348 --> 00:17:03,267
What I can tell you
is that it involved a major theft

290
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:06,395
from my clients, overseas

291
00:17:06,646 --> 00:17:07,772
two years ago.

292
00:17:08,314 --> 00:17:11,192
- And Fisher was connected how?
- He was a person of interest.

293
00:17:11,776 --> 00:17:15,071
Mr. Fisher left his job shortly after
an investigation was launched.

294
00:17:15,404 --> 00:17:17,907
His reasons for leaving
were... questionable.

295
00:17:18,199 --> 00:17:20,034
You said you had Fisher
under surveillance.

296
00:17:20,535 --> 00:17:22,662
So... where were you
the night of his death?

297
00:17:23,204 --> 00:17:24,914
I had information
that led me to believe

298
00:17:25,081 --> 00:17:27,375
that Mr. Fisher would be
at a different location.

299
00:17:28,209 --> 00:17:31,796
- Obviously, that was bad information.
- I guess.

300
00:17:32,964 --> 00:17:36,384
So... how did you come to be
in Fisher's locked shop

301
00:17:37,093 --> 00:17:38,219
carrying a weapon?

302
00:17:38,511 --> 00:17:41,264
I was surveilling the shop
when I heard a noise inside.

303
00:17:41,722 --> 00:17:43,516
The back door was open,
and I went in.

304
00:17:44,141 --> 00:17:45,851
Finding an armed man inside

305
00:17:45,977 --> 00:17:48,980
I drew my fully licensed
and permitted weapon.

306
00:17:49,939 --> 00:17:50,982
That's all.

307
00:17:51,482 --> 00:17:55,111
Now... my lawyer is already
on the way over

308
00:17:55,236 --> 00:17:58,698
but wouldn't we all rather
skip the formalities?

309
00:18:04,245 --> 00:18:07,957
- You getting anything?
- Well, they're both professional liars.

310
00:18:09,125 --> 00:18:11,002
Beyond that, I can't read either of them.

311
00:18:11,586 --> 00:18:12,628
They're both good for the murder

312
00:18:12,753 --> 00:18:14,672
so each gives the other
reasonable doubt.

313
00:18:15,214 --> 00:18:17,300
We need to come at this
from another angle.

314
00:18:18,342 --> 00:18:19,427
Just got off the phone
with Pete in forensics.

315
00:18:19,635 --> 00:18:21,762
He says the accent's Iraqi,
probably from Baghdad.

316
00:18:21,929 --> 00:18:25,349
- Any luck tracking down the caller?
- Prepaid cell phone. No subscriber info.

317
00:18:25,558 --> 00:18:27,059
I tried calling it,
but the minutes must be up.

318
00:18:27,310 --> 00:18:28,352
Great.

319
00:18:33,107 --> 00:18:34,150
Agent Lisbon.

320
00:18:34,650 --> 00:18:37,445
I just got a call from the State
Department about your case.

321
00:18:37,653 --> 00:18:40,364
- What should I know?
- The State Department? What'd they say?

322
00:18:40,489 --> 00:18:43,284
Our victim, Fisher, was suspected
of getting too close

323
00:18:43,409 --> 00:18:47,330
to shady Iraqi businessmen.
A theft may have occurred.

324
00:18:47,997 --> 00:18:49,165
Nothing could ever be proven.

325
00:18:50,041 --> 00:18:52,376
They would like to be kept abreast
of developments.

326
00:18:52,835 --> 00:18:53,878
We've got a threatening call

327
00:18:54,003 --> 00:18:55,713
from somebody
who may or may not be Iraqi

328
00:18:55,838 --> 00:18:57,048
and two suspects in holding

329
00:18:57,173 --> 00:18:58,924
who've got experience
with counterintelligence.

330
00:19:00,217 --> 00:19:02,762
And you have strong leads?
Something for me to tell Washington?

331
00:19:02,970 --> 00:19:04,889
Nothing concrete, sir.

332
00:19:05,014 --> 00:19:07,808
Early indicators say that it's revenge
for whatever happened in Iraq.

333
00:19:09,977 --> 00:19:11,562
What about you, Mr. Jane?

334
00:19:11,771 --> 00:19:13,898
What dazzling insights
have you gleaned?

335
00:19:15,024 --> 00:19:17,985
- I've got nothing.
- That's disappointing.

336
00:19:19,070 --> 00:19:22,448
- Off your game somehow? Distracted?
- No, not at all.

337
00:19:23,074 --> 00:19:25,993
Sorry to interrupt, boss, but I just talked
to the girlfriend, Heather blue.

338
00:19:26,285 --> 00:19:28,329
She's frightened. Says someone
is prowling around her apartment.

339
00:19:28,454 --> 00:19:29,497
Let's go and see her.

340
00:19:29,664 --> 00:19:30,831
I wanna ask her about
that Peace Corps thing anyway.

341
00:19:31,082 --> 00:19:32,750
Jane, are you co... Damn.

342
00:19:33,709 --> 00:19:36,170
- Problem?
- Not at all, sir.

343
00:19:36,504 --> 00:19:37,630
- Keep me informed.
- Okay.

344
00:19:38,881 --> 00:19:40,383
These people are lying to you.

345
00:19:40,925 --> 00:19:42,968
Teddy was in the Peace Corps.
I told you that.

346
00:19:43,803 --> 00:19:44,845
We checked.

347
00:19:45,054 --> 00:19:48,516
Ted Fisher worked for Sagaris
private defense from 2000 to 2009

348
00:19:49,433 --> 00:19:52,144
since he first got out of the army.
He was a mercenary.

349
00:19:52,311 --> 00:19:55,272
No. He wasn't.
Not possible.

350
00:19:56,273 --> 00:19:59,151
Heather, you called us here
because somebody frightened you.

351
00:19:59,902 --> 00:20:01,570
Oh, well, uh...

352
00:20:02,321 --> 00:20:05,199
Yeah, I was watching tv
when I heard the noise.

353
00:20:05,700 --> 00:20:06,742
He was trying
to come in the back

354
00:20:06,909 --> 00:20:09,328
- but when I yelled, he ran.
- Did you get a look at him?

355
00:20:10,162 --> 00:20:11,247
No, I just saw the glint

356
00:20:11,372 --> 00:20:13,165
of the knife he was using
to cut the screen.

357
00:20:13,416 --> 00:20:16,377
You can't remember anything else
about him? Size? Hair color?

358
00:20:17,002 --> 00:20:21,674
He was wearing a hooded sweatshirt, I think.
A little taller than me, maybe.

359
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:23,884
Are you sure it was male?
Could it have been a woman?

360
00:20:24,301 --> 00:20:27,179
I don't know. Maybe. God, my
heart's going a mile a minute.

361
00:20:27,596 --> 00:20:29,473
We've assigned you
a protection detail.

362
00:20:29,849 --> 00:20:31,016
Nobody's gonna hurt you.

363
00:20:33,227 --> 00:20:35,271
You really want me to believe
Ted lied to me?

364
00:20:36,939 --> 00:20:38,941
The whole time,
he was lying to me?

365
00:20:39,942 --> 00:20:40,985
I'm sorry.

366
00:20:52,705 --> 00:20:55,458
- O'Donnell. How goes it?
- Fine.

367
00:20:55,708 --> 00:20:58,294
As long as they keep bringing food
every couple hours, I'm good.

368
00:20:58,669 --> 00:20:59,837
Yeah. Well, I brought you a chair.

369
00:21:01,130 --> 00:21:02,339
Just opens up, and...

370
00:21:03,507 --> 00:21:04,633
- There.
- Great.

371
00:21:05,134 --> 00:21:06,594
Yeah, it's my pleasure.

372
00:21:07,553 --> 00:21:09,764
Just glad to know we got
a good man like you on the job.

373
00:21:09,930 --> 00:21:12,641
Just and ease your way into it. That's...
Just ease your way down.

374
00:21:13,267 --> 00:21:14,393
- Okay. There we go. Got it?
- Yeah.

375
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:17,980
Okay, fantastic. I'll check on you later on.

376
00:21:18,147 --> 00:21:19,940
Okay. Thanks.

377
00:21:30,701 --> 00:21:32,161
Whoever was trying
to get into the apartment

378
00:21:32,411 --> 00:21:33,913
it wasn't Ruger or Griswold.

379
00:21:34,205 --> 00:21:35,581
They were only released an hour ago.

380
00:21:35,998 --> 00:21:37,583
Well, maybe they have somebody else
working with them

381
00:21:37,917 --> 00:21:40,753
or the girlfriend is having
some very bad dreams.

382
00:21:41,754 --> 00:21:44,924
Okay. Do you have any intention
of actually taking part in this case?

383
00:21:47,218 --> 00:21:48,302
It's all under control.

384
00:21:49,762 --> 00:21:52,223
Wait. You know who the killer is?

385
00:21:53,224 --> 00:21:55,976
The what?
Oh, the killer. No. No, I misspoke.

386
00:21:56,811 --> 00:21:58,062
What's going on with you?

387
00:21:58,270 --> 00:22:00,314
You've been acting strange
since this case started.

388
00:22:00,523 --> 00:22:02,566
Strange. Strange how?

389
00:22:02,691 --> 00:22:03,734
Strange like...

390
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:05,402
Or...

391
00:22:06,028 --> 00:22:07,238
Boss, Rigsby's...

392
00:22:08,489 --> 00:22:09,532
Got something.

393
00:22:14,328 --> 00:22:15,621
Just got a call from
the guys in evidence.

394
00:22:15,746 --> 00:22:18,332
- Fisher's phone's was ringing.
- It's been going off every half-hour.

395
00:22:18,541 --> 00:22:20,960
- Where did the call come from?
- Same number... Prepaid cell phone.

396
00:22:21,168 --> 00:22:22,753
I thought you said the cell phone angle
was dead.

397
00:22:23,128 --> 00:22:24,421
He must've put more minutes on it.

398
00:22:29,176 --> 00:22:30,219
This is Cho.

399
00:22:30,803 --> 00:22:32,429
Yeah, we're all set here.
How are you guys coming along?

400
00:22:32,596 --> 00:22:34,557
Cho's talking to the phone company
so that when the call comes through

401
00:22:34,682 --> 00:22:36,016
we can triangulate off the cell towers

402
00:22:36,141 --> 00:22:37,726
figure out exactly where
the call came from.

403
00:22:39,937 --> 00:22:41,856
Okay, I need you to keep him on the phone
for at least two minutes.

404
00:22:43,691 --> 00:22:46,110
Hi. This is Patrick Jane.
Listen, I know who you are.

405
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:48,320
"Shut up! I am coming to get you."

406
00:22:48,571 --> 00:22:51,365
"I don't know who you are,
but I will make you pay for this!"

407
00:22:51,490 --> 00:22:52,741
No, you shut up. You listen to me.

408
00:22:52,867 --> 00:22:54,368
I know what you want.
The only way you're gonna get it

409
00:22:54,493 --> 00:22:56,370
is if you meet me in person.
We got something to settle here.

410
00:22:56,620 --> 00:22:58,622
Outside the repair shop in one hour.

411
00:22:58,747 --> 00:22:59,999
You be there you get what you want.

412
00:23:00,124 --> 00:23:02,459
If you don't you'll never see it again.
Simple as that.

413
00:23:04,628 --> 00:23:05,671
What was that?

414
00:23:05,796 --> 00:23:07,339
I said 2 minutes.
That was hardly 30 seconds.

415
00:23:07,673 --> 00:23:09,258
Well, I just thought it'd be simpler
to bring him to us.

416
00:23:10,801 --> 00:23:11,844
It's okay. Thank me later.

417
00:23:12,052 --> 00:23:13,304
Now the strangeness we were talking
about...

418
00:23:13,929 --> 00:23:15,431
- Never mind.
- Okay.

419
00:23:58,682 --> 00:23:59,725
Hey!

420
00:24:00,059 --> 00:24:01,101
No!

421
00:24:01,602 --> 00:24:02,645
Hey! Don't...

422
00:24:03,479 --> 00:24:06,732
Where is that bastard Fisher? No!

423
00:24:07,274 --> 00:24:09,818
Let me go!
Where is that bastard Fisher?

424
00:24:10,778 --> 00:24:13,113
- Who are you?
- Come on.

425
00:24:23,138 --> 00:24:26,266
- My name is Omar Hassan.
- Okay.

426
00:24:27,267 --> 00:24:28,727
Why were you so angry
with Ted Fisher?

427
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:32,439
My father worked
for the Americans

428
00:24:32,689 --> 00:24:35,567
helping to establish
a free and Democratic Iraq.

429
00:24:35,901 --> 00:24:37,319
He and Fisher worked together.

430
00:24:38,236 --> 00:24:39,279
Yes.

431
00:24:39,613 --> 00:24:41,865
- Before my father was killed.
- How'd he die?

432
00:24:44,201 --> 00:24:46,119
Bomb, two years ago.

433
00:24:46,703 --> 00:24:47,746
Fisher was involved
in his death?

434
00:24:48,288 --> 00:24:50,874
No, but when it became clear

435
00:24:51,166 --> 00:24:53,043
that my father's life
was in danger

436
00:24:53,835 --> 00:24:56,171
Fisher promised he would
take care of my family

437
00:24:56,338 --> 00:24:58,256
if anything happened
to my father.

438
00:24:58,632 --> 00:24:59,674
And Fisher told you this?

439
00:25:00,884 --> 00:25:04,221
After my father was killed,
Fisher quit his job.

440
00:25:05,138 --> 00:25:07,641
He...He wanted
no more of killing.

441
00:25:09,226 --> 00:25:12,187
He promised to help
get us to America

442
00:25:12,479 --> 00:25:16,316
and that the $2 million
would be waiting for us when we arrived.

443
00:25:16,608 --> 00:25:17,651
That's a lot of money.

444
00:25:18,443 --> 00:25:21,071
The State Department thinks Fisher
was engaged in criminal activity in Iraq.

445
00:25:21,279 --> 00:25:22,322
Is that where the money came from?

446
00:25:23,615 --> 00:25:27,410
No. It came from my father.
It was our family fortune.

447
00:25:27,661 --> 00:25:29,037
Your father put a lot of trust
in Fisher.

448
00:25:31,289 --> 00:25:32,415
He believed he was a good man.

449
00:25:35,085 --> 00:25:36,586
He stayed under our roof.

450
00:25:36,711 --> 00:25:39,005
He... He broke bread with us.

451
00:25:39,589 --> 00:25:42,467
So Fisher helped to get the visas
necessary to come to America

452
00:25:43,343 --> 00:25:45,262
and your family arrived four days ago.
Is that correct?

453
00:25:46,429 --> 00:25:47,848
And what happened when
you met up with Fisher?

454
00:25:48,765 --> 00:25:52,227
We were supposed to meet two nights ago
but he never showed up.

455
00:25:53,019 --> 00:25:55,230
You thought he double-crossed you
that he stole the money

456
00:25:56,606 --> 00:25:58,817
and that's why you cut the screen
and tried to break into the apartment.

457
00:26:00,986 --> 00:26:02,028
Yes.

458
00:26:04,030 --> 00:26:05,907
What about Fisher's friend,
Cole Ruger?

459
00:26:08,285 --> 00:26:10,370
Did you ever meet him or did Fisher
ever talk about him?

460
00:26:11,204 --> 00:26:12,914
No, I-I don't know him.

461
00:26:17,419 --> 00:26:18,795
What am I to tell my mother?

462
00:26:20,714 --> 00:26:21,756
My sisters?

463
00:26:23,758 --> 00:26:26,178
I mean, we have nothing now.

464
00:26:29,306 --> 00:26:30,348
Yeah. It's a tough break.

465
00:26:33,101 --> 00:26:34,144
I'll be right back.

466
00:26:36,229 --> 00:26:37,480
The kid doesn't seem
like a killer.

467
00:26:37,647 --> 00:26:39,524
I'm not sure the revenge
motive works.

468
00:26:40,901 --> 00:26:43,653
- What are you doing? What is that?
- None of your business.

469
00:26:43,778 --> 00:26:44,821
Are you even listening to me?

470
00:26:45,030 --> 00:26:46,615
Every word.
Let's go catch this killer, shall we?

471
00:26:46,823 --> 00:26:47,866
How are we gonna do that?

472
00:26:48,200 --> 00:26:51,203
The killer was looking for the Hassan
family's $2 million. The rest is easy.

473
00:26:57,417 --> 00:27:00,712
And now you want me to ask you
what the plan you worked out is don't you?

474
00:27:00,921 --> 00:27:03,632
No. I just want you to round everyone up
and get 'em to the repair shop.

475
00:27:03,757 --> 00:27:05,717
- Everyone? Who's everyone?
- Well, let me see.

476
00:27:05,842 --> 00:27:09,304
There's the girlfriend and Omar there
and, Griswold and Ruger.

477
00:27:09,429 --> 00:27:11,348
- I think that about covers it.
- Ruger and Griswold...

478
00:27:11,473 --> 00:27:12,724
They've already been released.

479
00:27:12,849 --> 00:27:15,894
And from what I've seen of the two of them
we're not really equipped to track 'em down.

480
00:27:16,019 --> 00:27:17,187
Oh, no. It's gonna be easy.

481
00:27:17,354 --> 00:27:19,606
Easy? Ruger bought a plane ticket
out of the country...

482
00:27:19,731 --> 00:27:21,399
The second he left the CBI.

483
00:27:21,524 --> 00:27:23,818
Griswold disappeared
even after we put a tail on her.

484
00:27:24,986 --> 00:27:27,364
Ruger is not leaving the country.
He is after the money.

485
00:27:27,489 --> 00:27:29,991
You heard Omar.
He'd never even heard of Ruger.

486
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:33,036
Ruger worked with Fisher, but I bet
they weren't friends.

487
00:27:33,286 --> 00:27:34,996
- And Griswold?
- Well, she's after the money, too.

488
00:27:35,205 --> 00:27:38,250
I expect they're both trying to get into
the repair shop as we speak.

489
00:27:38,583 --> 00:27:41,294
You just have to round them up.
Call me if you need any help.

490
00:27:41,419 --> 00:27:42,963
Wait a minute.
You're leaving again?

491
00:27:43,171 --> 00:27:44,214
What is going on with you?

492
00:27:44,422 --> 00:27:46,341
When have I ever
steered you wrong before?

493
00:27:48,468 --> 00:27:50,845
I mean, when have I ever
really steered you wrong

494
00:27:51,054 --> 00:27:53,056
where I've done something
that didn't work out

495
00:27:53,223 --> 00:27:54,891
- the way we wanted it to?
- Seriously?

496
00:27:55,058 --> 00:27:56,351
Okay, fine. Let me put it this way...

497
00:27:56,476 --> 00:27:58,687
When have I ever intentionally
screwed things up

498
00:27:58,853 --> 00:28:00,021
so that we couldn't solve the case?

499
00:28:02,065 --> 00:28:03,358
No. Not that I know of, at least.

500
00:28:03,525 --> 00:28:05,652
That's right. Thank you.
Have a little faith, Lisbon.

501
00:28:13,243 --> 00:28:15,453
Okay, Jane said
they're here somewhere.

502
00:28:16,204 --> 00:28:17,247
Let's take a look.

503
00:28:22,544 --> 00:28:25,297
- Probably not the homeless guy.
- No, Ruger tried that one before.

504
00:28:29,175 --> 00:28:30,218
How about the food guys?

505
00:28:31,219 --> 00:28:32,262
No, too short.

506
00:28:35,640 --> 00:28:36,683
Gotta be the truck?

507
00:28:37,517 --> 00:28:38,560
- That's it.
- Yep.

508
00:28:39,185 --> 00:28:41,521
- Hey, guys, I got this.
- Yes, you do.

509
00:28:56,953 --> 00:28:59,039
Hello Mr. Ruger.

510
00:28:59,581 --> 00:29:02,250
- How you're doing?
- What do you want?

511
00:29:02,709 --> 00:29:04,210
I'm not breaking any laws.

512
00:29:05,003 --> 00:29:07,797
We just wanted to ask you
into the repair shop.

513
00:29:08,840 --> 00:29:10,216
It would be a big help for us.

514
00:29:15,930 --> 00:29:17,724
All right. No problem.

515
00:29:26,232 --> 00:29:28,234
One down, one to go.

516
00:29:31,196 --> 00:29:32,822
- See anything?
- Nope.

517
00:29:44,542 --> 00:29:47,295
- "Cho, how's it going?"
- "We found Ruger in a delivery van"

518
00:29:47,670 --> 00:29:49,839
- "but Griswold's nowhere in sight."
- Are you near the repair shop?

519
00:29:50,215 --> 00:29:51,299
Yeah, right out front.

520
00:29:51,549 --> 00:29:53,343
Okay, from where you stand

521
00:29:53,468 --> 00:29:55,678
what is the best vantage point
to see the shop?

522
00:29:56,096 --> 00:29:58,431
He wants us to look for the best observation
post for watching the shop.

523
00:30:03,853 --> 00:30:04,896
How about way over there?

524
00:30:05,021 --> 00:30:06,064
That building right across the street?

525
00:30:06,189 --> 00:30:07,774
There's a building across the street.
Rooftop's good.

526
00:30:10,402 --> 00:30:12,779
Do you have a pen and some paper?

527
00:30:14,155 --> 00:30:15,532
What's up?
What are you doing?

528
00:30:18,827 --> 00:30:19,869
Throw me a pen.

529
00:30:20,120 --> 00:30:21,538
- A pen?
- Yeah.

530
00:30:23,164 --> 00:30:24,207
Nice catch.

531
00:30:37,011 --> 00:30:38,096
Oh, you've gotta be kidding me.

532
00:31:02,245 --> 00:31:03,955
Hey, O'Donnell, snack break.

533
00:31:04,539 --> 00:31:06,499
- Oh, thanks, buddy.
- No problem.

534
00:31:06,791 --> 00:31:07,834
It's about time.

535
00:31:10,170 --> 00:31:11,212
Turkey.

536
00:31:46,456 --> 00:31:48,750
Look, you gotta put this on.
We don't have a lot of time.

537
00:31:50,668 --> 00:31:52,462
You're gonna use this
to get out through the door.

538
00:31:59,969 --> 00:32:03,097
- Wait. What are you talking about?
- Well, you asked me to get you out of here.

539
00:32:03,223 --> 00:32:05,058
That's what I'm doing...Helping you escape.
Now hurry up.

540
00:32:05,183 --> 00:32:06,392
Escape? I'm not escaping.

541
00:32:06,518 --> 00:32:08,394
You think I want to spend
the rest of my life on the run?

542
00:32:08,686 --> 00:32:11,064
No. You get the charges dropped.
That's the deal.

543
00:32:11,981 --> 00:32:13,233
Hey, whoa, whoa.
I'm not a lawyer.

544
00:32:13,399 --> 00:32:15,318
I can't make charges
just disappear

545
00:32:15,527 --> 00:32:16,694
but I can get you to Mexico...

546
00:32:17,362 --> 00:32:18,696
Set you up with a little starter money.

547
00:32:18,863 --> 00:32:21,282
Mexico? Who the hell wants
to go to Mexico?

548
00:32:22,200 --> 00:32:24,202
You get me out free and legal,
or I start talking.

549
00:32:24,702 --> 00:32:25,995
I'm pretty sure the dirt I got on you

550
00:32:26,120 --> 00:32:27,830
will be worth
a "get out of jail free" card.

551
00:32:29,249 --> 00:32:30,291
You're very annoying.

552
00:32:32,126 --> 00:32:33,336
And you're running out of time.

553
00:32:43,513 --> 00:32:44,556
Are they the ones?

554
00:32:45,890 --> 00:32:46,975
Are you going to arrest them?

555
00:32:48,393 --> 00:32:50,687
Oh, hi. You must be Heather.
Thanks for coming by.

556
00:32:52,105 --> 00:32:53,314
Ted Fisher was a good man

557
00:32:53,439 --> 00:32:55,191
who kept trust
with the Hassan family.

558
00:32:55,316 --> 00:32:59,445
He was murdered over $2 million,
which is somewhere in this room.

559
00:32:59,988 --> 00:33:01,155
$2 million?

560
00:33:01,781 --> 00:33:03,616
Ted didn't have $2 million.

561
00:33:03,825 --> 00:33:06,411
Uh, yes he did.
They could tell you.

562
00:33:06,953 --> 00:33:08,079
How do you know it's in this room?

563
00:33:08,371 --> 00:33:11,791
Because we've checked every other location
connected to Fisher and so have you.

564
00:33:12,208 --> 00:33:13,293
That's why you're here.

565
00:33:13,585 --> 00:33:16,963
Hasn't this room already been searched
both by the killer and by the police?

566
00:33:17,130 --> 00:33:20,425
Yes, but they...I'm sorry...
Weren't creative enough.

567
00:33:20,758 --> 00:33:22,594
It could be in bearer bonds
hidden in the bills

568
00:33:22,760 --> 00:33:24,095
or invoices or files

569
00:33:24,220 --> 00:33:26,222
or more likely,
in the form of jewels

570
00:33:26,556 --> 00:33:29,976
hidden somewhere inside
these electronics.

571
00:33:30,310 --> 00:33:33,021
Whatever form it's in,
we're gonna find it right now.

572
00:33:33,229 --> 00:33:34,272
Come on in, folks.

573
00:33:34,689 --> 00:33:36,941
These people are gonna take everything
and pull it apart

574
00:33:37,150 --> 00:33:39,027
until they find the hidden treasure.

575
00:33:39,444 --> 00:33:42,822
Yes, piece by piece

576
00:33:43,406 --> 00:33:44,616
until we find it.

577
00:33:45,033 --> 00:33:46,075
Thank you, folks.

578
00:33:46,200 --> 00:33:48,119
If everybody could just step outside
and let these people do their jobs.

579
00:33:48,244 --> 00:33:49,621
- Thanks.
- Thank you.

580
00:34:10,933 --> 00:34:12,101
They didn't find anything.

581
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:15,063
No cash, no jewels,
no bonds. Nothin'.

582
00:34:16,481 --> 00:34:17,774
What am I going to do?

583
00:34:19,609 --> 00:34:21,110
My family is ruined.

584
00:34:22,070 --> 00:34:23,863
All my father did
was for nothing.

585
00:34:29,994 --> 00:34:31,037
What a waste of time.

586
00:34:32,413 --> 00:34:34,540
I'd agree with him, except
it's this kind of incompetence

587
00:34:34,666 --> 00:34:37,335
that makes people pay me
to produce results.

588
00:34:46,052 --> 00:34:47,762
These people have all been lying to you.

589
00:34:49,013 --> 00:34:50,264
I was right about Teddy.

590
00:34:51,683 --> 00:34:52,725
You were wrong.

591
00:34:54,769 --> 00:34:55,812
I figured it out.

592
00:34:57,230 --> 00:34:58,606
I know exactly
where the fortune is.

593
00:35:14,787 --> 00:35:15,830
Where is it then?

594
00:35:17,832 --> 00:35:18,874
Where's the money?

595
00:35:22,670 --> 00:35:23,713
You're on top of it.

596
00:35:25,715 --> 00:35:26,757
Under the carpet?

597
00:35:27,133 --> 00:35:30,177
No. It is the carpet.
Touch it.

598
00:35:30,553 --> 00:35:32,513
Pure silk, hand-knotted.

599
00:35:35,266 --> 00:35:37,184
It looks like
a dirty old carpet.

600
00:35:37,309 --> 00:35:39,812
Oh, yes, it's very old.
One of the reasons it's so valuable.

601
00:35:40,187 --> 00:35:42,732
- I gotta phone this in.
- Stop.

602
00:35:44,942 --> 00:35:45,985
Let's talk about this.

603
00:35:48,571 --> 00:35:53,075
If you did call it in the legal mess
will take forever to sort out.

604
00:35:54,243 --> 00:35:56,871
This carpet will just end up in
a government locker somewhere.

605
00:35:57,079 --> 00:35:58,122
Yeah, probably.

606
00:36:00,249 --> 00:36:01,542
What if we just...

607
00:36:02,418 --> 00:36:03,461
Took it?

608
00:36:04,712 --> 00:36:05,755
You and me?

609
00:36:09,925 --> 00:36:11,051
We took the carpet

610
00:36:12,261 --> 00:36:15,556
sold it, and divided
the profits between us?

611
00:36:17,224 --> 00:36:18,267
Exactly.

612
00:36:24,565 --> 00:36:25,608
That's, uh...

613
00:36:28,778 --> 00:36:29,820
That's a great idea.

614
00:36:30,321 --> 00:36:32,698
- Isn't it?
- 50/50 split?

615
00:36:33,240 --> 00:36:35,367
- Sure. 50/50.
- Deal.

616
00:36:38,954 --> 00:36:39,997
Deal.

617
00:36:40,206 --> 00:36:41,916
What did Ted say when you
suggested that deal to him?

618
00:36:43,292 --> 00:36:44,835
Ted was a softheaded fool.

619
00:36:47,588 --> 00:36:48,672
So you knew about the money.

620
00:36:49,673 --> 00:36:51,008
You knew Ted wasn't
with the Peace Corps.

621
00:36:51,133 --> 00:36:53,385
- You knew everything.
- I didn't...I didn't say that.

622
00:36:53,511 --> 00:36:58,140
Oh, yes you did which means, Heather,
it was you that shot Ted.

623
00:36:58,349 --> 00:37:00,643
He told you about his identity,
he told you about the money

624
00:37:00,810 --> 00:37:04,605
- and you shot him for it.
- No. Uh, that's...No!

625
00:37:04,772 --> 00:37:07,983
Well, forgive me if I...
I'm not quite convinced.

626
00:37:08,400 --> 00:37:09,443
But I do have to phone this in.

627
00:37:09,568 --> 00:37:11,362
- Lisbon'll be very happy to know...
- Stop.

628
00:37:13,239 --> 00:37:14,782
Are you pointing a gun at me,
Heather?

629
00:37:15,491 --> 00:37:17,993
The man I loved was
in the Peace Corps.

630
00:37:18,369 --> 00:37:20,037
He wanted to make the world
a better place.

631
00:37:20,329 --> 00:37:22,414
Ted wasn't who he said he was.

632
00:37:22,832 --> 00:37:24,625
He was a soldier, a killer.

633
00:37:24,792 --> 00:37:26,627
So you shot him?
That's a little counterintuitive.

634
00:37:26,752 --> 00:37:27,795
He lied to me!

635
00:37:28,003 --> 00:37:30,422
This had nothing to do
with the $2 million?

636
00:37:30,548 --> 00:37:31,590
You can't understand.

637
00:37:31,715 --> 00:37:34,301
You can only see things from
your bourgeois capitalist mindset.

638
00:37:34,552 --> 00:37:37,638
Do you know how much good
could be done with $2 million?!

639
00:37:37,847 --> 00:37:40,015
How many animals could be saved?!

640
00:37:40,391 --> 00:37:42,601
But you couldn't find the money.
The clock was ticking.

641
00:37:42,810 --> 00:37:44,895
You looked everywhere,
and you couldn't find it.

642
00:37:45,145 --> 00:37:46,689
He caught me
searching the shop.

643
00:37:47,273 --> 00:37:49,233
He talked to me like
I was some kind of criminal.

644
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:51,861
Well, uh, pretty much what you are.

645
00:37:52,278 --> 00:37:55,197
No. I'm blessed is what I am.

646
00:37:55,656 --> 00:37:57,616
The universe wants me
to have this money.

647
00:37:59,535 --> 00:38:01,287
I'm sorry, I have to kill you

648
00:38:01,912 --> 00:38:03,622
but I'm doing it from a place of love.

649
00:38:04,206 --> 00:38:06,083
Drop the gun.
Put your hands on your head.

650
00:38:07,334 --> 00:38:08,377
Oh, wow.

651
00:38:11,630 --> 00:38:12,923
I'm gonna leave you with that.

652
00:38:23,726 --> 00:38:25,436
You were right about Ruger
and Griswold.

653
00:38:25,853 --> 00:38:26,896
He's already disappeared

654
00:38:27,021 --> 00:38:29,106
and she's admitted she was working
for the State Department

655
00:38:29,231 --> 00:38:31,442
who suspected Fisher
of smuggling and theft.

656
00:38:32,067 --> 00:38:34,612
But they've agreed to recognize
the Hassan family's right to the rug

657
00:38:34,778 --> 00:38:36,447
so everything ends happily.

658
00:38:38,157 --> 00:38:39,199
Good.

659
00:38:39,325 --> 00:38:41,201
I waited for you to tell the Hassans.
Come on.

660
00:38:41,452 --> 00:38:42,536
Oh, no, thanks.

661
00:38:43,871 --> 00:38:46,290
- What do you mean, "no, thanks"?
- Well, uh, you know

662
00:38:46,415 --> 00:38:48,000
I don't have time
for that kind of treacle.

663
00:38:48,584 --> 00:38:50,085
I have to rearrange
my sock drawer.

664
00:38:54,882 --> 00:38:56,383
I have a serious problem
I have to solve

665
00:38:56,508 --> 00:38:58,302
and I don't have a lot of time
to solve it in.

666
00:39:00,930 --> 00:39:02,765
I knew something
was going on with you.

667
00:39:04,975 --> 00:39:06,018
What is it?

668
00:39:09,939 --> 00:39:12,149
The absolute truth,
or I beat it outta you.

669
00:39:13,275 --> 00:39:15,903
Laroche has something I need,
so I hired Culpepper

670
00:39:16,028 --> 00:39:17,488
to break into Laroche's house.

671
00:39:17,863 --> 00:39:20,991
If I can't get the charges dropped
Culpepper is gonna tell on me.

672
00:39:24,036 --> 00:39:25,412
Oh... my God.

673
00:39:25,621 --> 00:39:26,997
Yeah, it's not good.

674
00:39:27,498 --> 00:39:29,208
What does Laroche have
that you want?

675
00:39:33,045 --> 00:39:34,380
Hightower didn't murder anybody.

676
00:39:35,089 --> 00:39:36,215
She was framed.

677
00:39:36,632 --> 00:39:38,968
Red John has a friend inside the CBI.

678
00:39:40,594 --> 00:39:41,637
The Todd Johnson murder.

679
00:39:42,012 --> 00:39:44,390
Laroche had a short list of suspects
that led to Hightower.

680
00:39:44,515 --> 00:39:46,517
That's what you were
trying to steal...The list.

681
00:39:46,934 --> 00:39:50,020
Yes. There were four names
on that list besides Hightower's.

682
00:39:50,145 --> 00:39:53,232
- One of them is working for red John.
- Did you ask Laroche?

683
00:39:53,357 --> 00:39:55,901
Laroche? No. Because if
it's not one of those names

684
00:39:56,110 --> 00:39:57,778
then Laroche is Red John's man.

685
00:39:58,195 --> 00:39:59,989
Why didn't you tell me about this earlier?

686
00:40:00,990 --> 00:40:02,199
I don't want to put you in danger.

687
00:40:02,574 --> 00:40:03,617
You're an idiot.

688
00:40:04,159 --> 00:40:07,329
I will fix this but we are gonna have
a serious talk after.

689
00:40:07,496 --> 00:40:08,539
Fix it?

690
00:40:08,664 --> 00:40:10,916
I've been trying to fix it all weekend.
I had the perfect escape worked out

691
00:40:11,041 --> 00:40:13,168
and then Culpepper says,
"no, I want the charges dropped"

692
00:40:13,293 --> 00:40:14,628
Yeah, yeah. Let's go.

693
00:40:22,219 --> 00:40:23,262
Open it up.

694
00:40:24,054 --> 00:40:26,306
- He's in there with his lawyer.
- Perfect.

695
00:40:34,940 --> 00:40:38,694
Lady cop. Okay. I can get behind that.

696
00:40:39,361 --> 00:40:41,947
A dumb cop who's pretty
is better than a dumb cop who ain't...

697
00:40:43,490 --> 00:40:45,868
What do you think you're doing here?!
You...You can't do that.

698
00:40:46,452 --> 00:40:49,747
That's a violation of due process.
You just got this case dismissed.

699
00:41:02,801 --> 00:41:04,762
"What were you thinking Agent Lisbon?"

700
00:41:05,387 --> 00:41:08,182
Due to your actions,
a criminal is going free.

701
00:41:10,100 --> 00:41:12,519
I'm sorry, sir.
The frustrations of the job...

702
00:41:12,644 --> 00:41:15,189
Your apologies
are of no import, agent.

703
00:41:15,564 --> 00:41:18,025
Donald Culpepper can no longer
be brought to trial.

704
00:41:18,275 --> 00:41:20,319
Well, hang on.
He didn't actually steal anything, did he?

705
00:41:23,572 --> 00:41:26,200
The 1-week suspension is mandatory.

706
00:41:26,742 --> 00:41:29,828
Also, six months
of anger management classes.

707
00:41:32,956 --> 00:41:33,999
Yes, sir.

708
00:41:38,629 --> 00:41:41,465
- I thought that went well.
- You're gonna owe me big-time.

709
00:41:41,799 --> 00:41:44,468
- Uh, for what exactly?
- Or I will...

710
00:41:44,635 --> 00:41:47,137
Two words...
Anger management.

711
00:41:50,140 --> 00:41:51,183
Hey.

712
00:41:52,643 --> 00:41:53,685
Thank you.
